---
import { md } from "@daviddef/archive-kit/lib/md.js";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Evidence from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Evidence.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import stats from "../data/stats.json";
import audit from "../data/audit-negatives.json";
import instruments from "../data/instruments.json";

/* This page used to say «nine were audited and five had already failed» while
   the page it linked to counted for itself. Two more failed on 20 September and
   the sentence went on saying five. It counts now. */
const wrong = audit.filter((r) => r.outcome === "WRONG").length;
const WORD = ["no","one","two","three","four","five","six","seven","eight","nine","ten","eleven","twelve","thirteen"];
const word = (n) => WORD[n] || String(n);
---
<Base title="Method" eyebrow="How this archive decides what it knows"
      dek="An archive is only worth reading if it says which of its claims are documents, which are inferences, and which are things the family remembers. These are the rules, and they are the same rules as The Falco Archive.">


  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Added 14 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Every negative in this archive, audited</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Twice in one week a search that had been closed turned out to be open. So each negative was
      re-read — not to repeat it, but to ask <strong>what set its window</strong>. {word(audit.length).charAt(0).toUpperCase() + word(audit.length).slice(1)} have been audited
      and <strong>{word(wrong)} had already failed</strong>, not because the reading was careless but because
      the window came from a declared age or an assumed eldest child, and the search then honoured
      that window exactly.</p>
      <p>The rule that falls out of it, and the {word(audit.length - wrong)} negatives still standing in the same shape, are
      on <a href={u("/audit")}><strong>The Audit</strong></a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" id="instruments">
    <div class="lab">The instruments</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">How this archive reads, as opposed to what it found</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>A <strong>finding</strong> belongs to a person and lives on
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Corrections</a> or <a href={u("/searched/")}>What Has Been Read</a>.
      An <strong>instrument</strong> belongs to a record series, and will be wanted by whoever reads it
      next — including the other six archives in this estate, which use the same registers in different
      towns. Every one of these was learnt twice before it was written down once.</p>
      <p class="note"><strong>Every instrument here carries a caution, and that is not decoration.</strong>
      A method described without the way it fails is how a method becomes a superstition. The annual
      Tavola is the best tool in this archive <em>and</em> it changes its alphabetical key between
      adjacent years, which has already produced one confident, complete and entirely false negative.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec" style="margin-top:20px">
      {instruments.map((i) => (
        <>
          <div class="k">{i.where}</div>
          <div class="v">
            <strong>{i.instrument}</strong> — <Fragment set:html={md(i.what)} />
            <div class="small" style="margin-top:.4rem" set:html={md(i.how)} />
            <div class="note small" style="margin-top:.5rem"><strong>Caution.</strong> <Fragment set:html={md(i.caution)} /></div>
          </div>
          <div class="w"></div>
        </>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" id="ladder">
    <h2>The five words</h2>
    <p class="prose">All seven of these family archives grade a claim with the same five words, so a
    level means the same thing wherever you meet it. The word is always printed next to the colour,
    because a reader should never have to know the palette to know the confidence.</p>
    <dl class="ladder">
      <dt><Evidence level="documented" /></dt>
      <dd>A record was read, and it says this. The source is named.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="probable" /></dt>
      <dd>The evidence points one way and nothing found so far points the other. Not proven.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="inferred" /></dt>
      <dd>Reasoned from what is known — a gap of the right size, a name in the right place — rather
      than witnessed in a record.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="family" /></dt>
      <dd>Told, remembered, or written on a family chart. Kept, because discarding it would lose the
      lead; labelled, because it is not evidence.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="disputed" /></dt>
      <dd>Two sources disagree and neither has been ruled out. Both are shown.</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="notclaimed">
      <span class="lab">Not claimed</span>
      <p class="prose">Some pages carry this instead. It states the thing a reader is likely to
      assume — the connection that would be tidy, the identification that almost works — and says
      plainly that this archive does not assert it. An archive is judged as much by what it refuses
      to say as by what it says.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="blk">
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px">Almost everything in this archive currently sits at the
    <strong>family</strong> level of the ladder above: <em>asserted by the family tree</em>, and not
    yet by a document. The tree this site was built from is a MyHeritage tree contributed by Michael Mazza and
    relatives. Where it cites Ancestry indexes, this site says so; where it cites nothing — which is
    most of it — this site says that too. Converting those assertions into documents is the work.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" id="living">
    <h2>Living people, and who counts as one</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p><strong>A living person (80 or younger, with no death recorded) is named with their
      relationships and their town and country, and nothing more.</strong> They get a page of their
      own, so that no name here is a dead end, but the page has no dates, no records, no sources and
      no photograph; it carries no structured data and it is kept out of search engines and the
      sitemap. A name page whose every bearer is living is kept out in the same way. This is the
      estate's one rule, set by David on 16 October 2026 and applied by the same code in every
      archive.</p>
      <p>Who is living is decided in one place, <code>tools/living_v2.py</code>, which hands each
      person's facts to the kit's <code>living_rule.py</code>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>80 or younger</strong>, counting by year (this year less the birth year), with
        <strong>no death or burial recorded</strong> anywhere this archive has read &mdash; the tree
        and the registers alike. A recorded death or burial, dated or not, means dead.</li>
        <li><strong>A public record source</strong> (FamilySearch, MyHeritage record collections,
        Findmypast and the others in the kit's list) naming the person takes them out of the living,
        and what that source says may be shown. A private family-tree entry does not count.</li>
        <li><strong>No birth year</strong> means the person cannot be shown to be 80 or younger, so
        they are not living by this test. That is why {stats.people - stats.living} of this
        archive's {stats.people} people are ordinary pages: most of this tree is <em>undated</em>,
        not <em>alive</em>, and only {stats.withDeath} have a death recorded anywhere. The older
        test (&ldquo;the tree cannot show they died&rdquo;) treated 411 people as possibly alive, and
        280 of them were never living at all: 263 are now ordinary pages, written up in full, and
        17 are undated children of living parents, who are held out.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A child with a known birth year follows the same rule as any living person: named, with
      relationships and their town and country, on a page with no dates. The only people <strong>not
      published at all</strong> are those with no birth year, no recorded death and no public source
      who have a living parent, because they <strong>may be a child</strong>: no page, and no name as
      anyone&rsquo;s relative. {stats.heldOut} people are held out. A living person&rsquo;s town is cut back to the town and country if the
      tree gives anything finer, such as a hospital or a street.</p>
      <p>The living person&rsquo;s fields are built from name, relationships and place only; their
      dates, sources and photographs never enter any file this repository publishes, because every
      committed file here is public. The build refuses to ship if that is ever untrue
      (<code>check:living</code> and <code>check:living-v2</code>).</p>
      <p>A removal request is honoured within days, without argument and without requiring a reason.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Why the archive refuses to merge {stats.unresolvedNames} names</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The export contains four men called <strong>Rosario Mazza</strong>, four called
      <strong>Giuseppe Arena</strong>, three called <strong>Orazio Mazza</strong> and four called
      <strong>Maria Mazza</strong> — most of them with no date of any kind. In three southern Italian
      towns where the same fifteen forenames circulate for two centuries, and where a grandson is
      named for his grandfather by rule, that is exactly what you would expect.</p>
      <p>An earlier version of this build merged people on <em>name and name alone</em>. It fused four
      unrelated Rosario Mazza stubs into one man and produced an <strong>eleven-generation Mazza
      descent</strong> that no record supports — a pedigree manufactured by a bug. The rule now is:</p>
      <blockquote>A shared name is not evidence. Two records are the same person only if they give the
      <strong>same birth year</strong>, or if they sit in the <strong>same place in the family</strong> —
      the same parents, or the same spouse and children. Anything less is recorded as an open question
      and left as two people.</blockquote>
      <p>Under that rule {stats.duplicates} genuine duplicates were merged — including the whole of
      Frank Mazza's household, which the tree holds twice — and
      <strong>{stats.unresolvedNames} shared names were left standing apart</strong>. The archive would
      rather be visibly incomplete than invisibly wrong.</p>

      <h3>And one way the rule was too strict</h3>
      <p>A rule that keys on the exact name is defeated by a <strong>single middle initial</strong>.
      The tree holds <em>Joseph A Polistena</em> and <em>Joseph Polistena</em> as two men — same wife,
      <strong>same full death date, 15 February 2012</strong>, same grave at Arneytown, New Jersey.
      It also holds <em>Michael Mazza</em> and <em>Michael Rocco Mazza</em>, same parents, the same birth year. One man each time, and the first version of this build refused to join either.</p>
      <p>So names are compared a second time at a looser key — <strong>surname plus first
      forename</strong>, middle names and initials dropped — and because that test is weaker, the
      corroboration demanded is stronger: an identical <em>full</em> death date, or identical parents
      <em>and</em> identical spouse, or the same birth year plus one of those. Across the whole export
      that widened rule found exactly <strong>two</strong> further people, and both are named on this
      page rather than merged quietly.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>One tree, or {stats.components.length} pieces?</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.8rem">The export is not a tree. It is
    <strong>{stats.components.length} disconnected components</strong>: one of {stats.components[0]}
    people, one of {stats.components[1]}, and twelve fragments. A family-tree program will draw them on
    one canvas and let you believe they are one family. This site labels every person with the
    component they belong to and refuses to imply a join that no record makes.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>What it would take to join them →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
